{
  "configuration_id": "config_024",
  "reason": "Evidence: The best result so far is config_022 (multimodal, single-profile, PCA+ViT-L, concat) at 0.535 accuracy, showing multimodal fusion provides a ~5% gain over RNA alone (0.485) and that ViT-L is the stronger image encoder. However, all completed experiments use single-profile; the multi-profile dimension (with aggregation) is completely untested and represents the largest source of uncertainty about potential performance gains. Question: Does aggregating multiple profiles (via avgpool) on top of the best-performing multimodal pipeline improve accuracy beyond 0.535? Why this config: config_024 is the most direct, minimal extension of config_022—same RNA encoder (PCA), same image encoder (ViT-L), same fusion (concat)—changing only profile setting to multi-profile with the simplest aggregation (avgpool). This isolates the effect of multi-profile aggregation, reducing uncertainty about whether richer profile representation matters, before committing budget to more complex variants (transformer aggregation, different encoders). If it helps, it opens a promising direction; if not, it efficiently rules out that axis and redirects effort toward RNA encoder choice (SCVI/scGPT)."
}

